 2. Tesla auto&#x27;s big farewell 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photo: David Paul Morris/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Elon Musk's spectacular unveiling of a flying, rocket-powered Tesla Roadster last night could be seen as a boisterous farewell to Tesla, the car company, Axios' Joann Muller writes .

 Zoom in: The Roadster is an extravagant tribute to the very thing Tesla is moving beyond: the thrill of driving.

 Musk told the audience the two-seater would be "the only manually driven car that Tesla makes" in the long term. And last night's demo arguably had little to do with selling them. Not every version of the Roadster will be street legal. When someone shouted that question from the crowd, Musk replied with a playful joke. 👁️ And he even had some fun while acknowledging the risks of driving a rocket-powered automobile: "We'll just ride the limit of 'don't detach the retina of the human in the car.'"

 Reality check: For all its technological wizardry — a suction fan system and compressed-air thrusters — the Roadster isn't likely to generate much revenue for Tesla. Musk has said as much.

 The bottom line: Tesla is more of an AI and robotics company than an automaker now.

 The original Roadster announced Tesla's arrival as a car company. The new one might be its most extravagant exit. Go deeper 

 
 
